{
  "ideas": [
    {
      "title": "Integral Field Tomography for Quantitative Nanoscale Multiphysics Mapping at Operating Interfaces",
      "motivation": "Microscopic interface science is limited because each probe sees only one projection of a coupled interfacial state. Optical interference, ellipsometry, neutron reflectivity, and infrared spectroscopy can resolve film thickness, density, roughness, and molecular layering, but they do not directly expose local charge-transfer or flow heterogeneity [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308711154). In situ conductive AFM can map local conductivity, chemical-frictional response, and morphology at electrocatalyst-electrolyte interfaces, yet it does not by itself recover long-range concentration, temperature, or mass-flux fields [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595). Nanoscale directional flow mapping shows that liquid-solid interfacial flow can be imaged, but flow alone does not yield absolute coupled mass, heat, or electron flux [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404). Triple-phase catalysis further shows that wettability and mass transfer determine reactive performance, but mechanistic resolution remains partial [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347). The unresolved bottleneck is not another single instrument but a quantitative inverse problem that fuses heterogeneous observables into one interfacial field state. I adapt integral field tomography from living-systems imaging, which treats multiple measurements as projections of a common field and enforces cross-modality consistency [Author et al., 2026](https://openalex.org/W7128955655). The primary testable hypothesis is that projection-operator integral-field tomography recovers absolute local interfacial flux more accurately and with better uncertainty calibration than single-modality inversion or unstructured late fusion, under identical measurements and regularization.",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve the inability of single-modality interfacial measurements to provide quantitative, co-registered nanoscale fields of structure, chemistry, flow, charge transfer, and flux at operating interfaces; it can be divided into 4 parts: (1) a correlative operando measurement protocol, (2) modality-specific forward projection models, (3) an integral-field tomographic inversion that enforces cross-modality consistency and conservation laws, and (4) flux extraction, validation, and scale-up.",
      "proposed_method": [
        {
          "name": "Correlative operando interface cell",
          "description": "Build a fiducialized microcell that permits the same interfacial region to be observed under operating conditions by structural and spectroscopic probes such as simultaneous neutron reflectivity, infrared spectroscopy, and ellipsometry [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308711154), by in situ conductive AFM for morphology, conductivity, and chemical-frictional contrast [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595), and by directional liquid-solid flow mapping [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404). For gas-liquid-solid studies, add wettability and mass-transfer characterization to define the triple-phase microenvironment [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347). The cell uses environmental control, low-perturbation probes, and fiducial markers so that data can be co-registered without assuming that one modality can substitute for another."
        },
        {
          "name": "Projection-operator field model",
          "description": "Represent the interface by latent fields: film thickness, composition or concentration, temperature, velocity, electron-transfer rate or local conductivity, and mass or heat flux. Treat each measurement as a projection of these fields rather than as an isolated map: reflectivity and ellipsometry constrain depth-integrated structural profiles [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308711154), infrared spectroscopy constrains molecular composition, AFM constrains near-surface electrical and chemical-frictional states [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595), directional flow imaging constrains tangential velocity orientation and magnitude [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404), and triple-phase wettability and mass-transfer measurements constrain boundary conditions [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347). This projection-operator representation is adapted from integral field tomography, where heterogeneous projections are unified into one reconstructed field [Author et al., 2026](https://openalex.org/W7128955655)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Integral-field tomographic inversion",
          "description": "Solve a joint spatiotemporal inverse problem that minimizes disagreement between measured and predicted projections while enforcing cross-modality consistency and relevant conservation laws for mass, species, energy, and charge. The inversion returns posterior fields and uncertainties instead of separate qualitative images. The core borrowed mechanism is integral field tomography: a unified multi-projection inverse problem with explicit forward models and consistency constraints, rather than sequential thresholding or ad hoc image overlay [Author et al., 2026](https://openalex.org/W7128955655). In reactive or electrochemical cases, local conductivity and electron-transfer heterogeneity are included as coupled fields, consistent with operando conductive AFM observations [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Absolute flux extraction and multiscale validation",
          "description": "Convert reconstructed gradients, boundary conditions, and interfacial resistances into absolute local mass, heat, and charge fluxes using the same transport equations used in the inversion. Validate local flux maps against independent totals such as gravimetric evaporation, electrochemical current, or calibrated heater power, and against functional interfacial descriptors such as triple-phase mass-transfer behavior [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347). This step connects nanoscale heterogeneity to device-relevant rates, addressing the gap between local interfacial observation and macroscopic evaporation, catalysis, spray drying, or cooling performance [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404) [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595)."
        }
      ],
      "analogy_mapping": [
        {
          "inspiration": "Toward Integral Field Tomography of Living Systems",
          "concept": "A unified multi-projection inverse problem that reconstructs a single field state from heterogeneous measurements using forward projection models and cross-modality consistency.",
          "adaptation": "Adapt to microscopic interfaces by defining interfacial thickness, composition, temperature, velocity, charge transfer, and flux as latent fields, and by treating neutron reflectivity, infrared spectroscopy, ellipsometry, AFM, directional flow mapping, and wettability measurements as projection operators. The reconstruction yields quantitative local fluxes and uncertainties rather than only separate images.",
          "citation": "[Author et al., 2026](https://openalex.org/W7128955655)"
        }
      ],
      "experiment_plan": [
        "Phase 1 primary hypothesis and single factor: test whether structured projection-operator integral-field tomography improves recovery of absolute local evaporation or mass flux compared with single-modality inversion and unstructured late fusion. The only intentionally varied factor is the fusion or inversion mechanism; the physical sample, measurement set, spatial registration, mesh, regularization budget, and solver time are held fixed.",
        "Build a controlled liquid-solid benchmark: a flat, fiducialized silica or gold substrate with a thin transparent microheater and a pure liquid film of fixed thickness and fixed humidity and temperature. Use one fixed spatial heating pattern to create a known, stable local evaporation or flux pattern. Ground truth is obtained from calibrated heater power, independent gravimetric mass loss, and finite-element heat and mass transport simulation validated by the same fiducials.",
        "Acquire the fixed multimodal data on the same region: film thickness or structural profile by ellipsometry or reflectivity-compatible optical measurement [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308711154); directional interfacial flow by nanoscale liquid-solid flow mapping [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404); local morphology, conductivity, or chemical-frictional contrast by in situ conductive AFM where an electrochemical or conductive layer is present [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595); and boundary wettability or mass-transfer context by standard contact-angle and mass-transfer characterization [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347).",
        "Reconstruction comparison for the primary hypothesis: apply (i) each modality alone, (ii) independent modality-specific inversions followed by unstructured late fusion, (iii) randomized projection fusion in which measurement-to-field projection operators or spatial registrations are shuffled, and (iv) structured integral-field tomography. All methods use the same latent-field mesh, noise model, regularization strength selected from a held-out validation subset, and conservation constraints where mathematically applicable, so structured projection consistency is the isolated active factor.",
        "Controls for algorithmic confounders: the single-modality reconstructions test whether one component alone can explain the result; the late-fusion condition tests whether simply combining data without a common field model is sufficient; the randomized projection condition tests whether improvement comes from structured forward models rather than extra parameters or regularization; and noise-matched synthetic data with known fields tests whether the inversion recovers ground truth rather than fitting noise.",
        "Characterization to rule out trivial confounders: measure and report registration error using fiducials and repeat reconstructions with perturbed registration; match solver complexity and parameter counts across methods; use blank no-heater and uniform-heater runs to exclude bulk drift, humidity changes, or thermal expansion as the source of apparent flux patterns; use low-force AFM settings and pre-run and post-run optical thickness checks to exclude probe-induced film perturbation; include surface roughness and morphology as measured covariates to exclude topography-only explanations [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595).",
        "Decision criterion for Phase 1: accept the primary hypothesis only if structured integral-field tomography yields statistically lower local flux error and better calibrated uncertainty than each single modality, late fusion, and randomized projection on the same data, and if the improvement persists under registration perturbation and noise-matched synthetic benchmarks.",
        "Phase 2 secondary mechanism tests, after Phase 1 passes: vary one secondary factor at a time, such as adding a gas phase to create a gas-liquid-solid triple line, changing substrate wettability, or introducing an electrochemical reaction. Use the same integral-field tomography pipeline to ask whether local wetting patches, electron-transfer hot spots, or concentration gradients causally alter local flux [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347) [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595).",
        "Phase 3 operando scope: apply the validated method to more realistic evaporative cooling, catalytic, or spray-drying-relevant interfaces, using simultaneous structural and spectroscopic probes where available [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308711154) and correlative AFM or flow probes where lateral resolution is needed [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404) [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595). Compare reconstructed local fluxes with macroscopic device metrics such as total evaporation rate, current density, or cooling power.",
        "Negative-result interpretation: if structured integral-field tomography does not outperform controls, the hypothesis is refuted or the forward models are insufficient; the next step would be to improve projection operators or measurement registration rather than adding more modalities."
      ],
