{
  "ideas": [
    {
      "title": "Reciprocal-Response Interfacial Data Assimilation for Measuring Coupled Microscopic Fluxes at Operating Interfaces",
      "motivation": "Current microscopic interface measurements are modality-limited: simultaneous neutron reflectivity, infrared spectroscopy, and ellipsometry can resolve molecular-length-scale structure and chemistry at solid-liquid boundaries [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308711154), in situ conductive atomic force microscopy can map local electron-transfer heterogeneity [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595), triple-phase methods can characterize wettability and mass transfer [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347), and directional-flow mapping can reveal tangential liquid-solid flow [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404). These advances still leave the central measurement bottleneck: no approach converts partial observables into absolute local coupled fluxes or local transport coefficients under operating conditions. The proposed idea transfers inverse-problem and data-assimilation mechanisms from geophysical hidden-state estimation [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W2898008281) and multiscale chemical transport [Author et al., 2007](https://openalex.org/W2112748697) to interfacial science. It adds a nonequilibrium thermodynamic constraint, namely Onsager reciprocity and non-negative local entropy production, so the inverse solver recovers interfacial constitutive coefficients rather than only smoothed fields. Interface-selective molecular tracing is used to isolate weak interfacial signals from bulk background [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4390232513).",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve the inability of current microscopic interface measurements to quantify coupled mass, heat, charge, and momentum transfer at operating interfaces; it can be divided into 5 parts: an active perturbation cell, co-registered multimodal observables, a nonequilibrium flux-force forward model, an observability-guided data-assimilation inverse solver, and a causal validation module. The primary testable hypothesis is that an inversion constrained by Onsager reciprocity and non-negative local entropy production will recover local interfacial transport coefficients and predict held-out fluxes more accurately than unconstrained fusion of the same observables. The approach therefore measures interface phenomena by identifying local constitutive laws, not merely by producing separate structural, optical, or electrical images.",
      "proposed_method": [
        {
          "name": "Active reciprocal perturbation interface cell",
          "description": "A planar microcell imposes small, localized thermal, electric, and chemical-potential perturbations on a liquid-solid or gas-liquid-solid interface while holding geometry, film thickness, and bulk composition fixed. The perturbations are small enough to remain in a linear-response regime around the operating steady state. This converts passive observation into an active identifiability experiment, in the spirit of directional liquid-solid flow mapping [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404) and operando triple-phase mass-transfer characterization [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Co-registered multimodal interfacial observables",
          "description": "The cell measures interfacial temperature and concentration with interface-selective luminescent reporters [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4390232513), tangential flow with tracer or velocimetry methods related to nanoscale directional flow mapping [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3028689404), local charge response with microelectrodes or in situ conductive atomic force microscopy [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595), and structural baseline with simultaneous reflectivity, spectroscopy, or ellipsometry where accessible [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308711154)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Nonequilibrium flux-force forward model",
          "description": "A thin-interface conservation model relates local fluxes of heat, mass, charge, and tangential momentum to local thermodynamic forces through a coefficient matrix L(x). The model enforces conservation of energy, species, charge, and momentum at the interface, non-negative local entropy production, and Onsager reciprocal cross-coefficients for small perturbations around steady state. This makes the unknowns physically meaningful constitutive quantities, such as local thermal conductance or resistance, electro-osmotic mobility, diffusio-osmotic mobility, Marangoni stress coefficient, and interfacial reaction conductance or rate constant, rather than generic image intensities. The need to connect wettability, flow, and mass transfer motivates the coupled flux-force representation [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347), while local electron-transfer observables motivate the charge-coupling terms [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4321480595)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Observability-guided data-assimilation inverse solver",
          "description": "The inverse problem is solved by adapting data-assimilation frameworks from inverse problems and Earth-system state estimation [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W2898008281) and multiscale chemical transport models [Author et al., 2007](https://openalex.org/W2112748697). A variational or ensemble estimator fuses time-resolved observables with the forward model, propagates uncertainty, and uses observability analysis to select perturbation frequencies and locations. The Onsager and entropy-production constraints are imposed as structured physical priors, not as generic smoothing, and the observability analysis explicitly identifies which latent interfacial fields are recoverable from which perturbation and measurement combinations."
        },
        {
          "name": "Causal validation and scale-up module",
          "description": "Recovered coefficients are tested by predicting responses to perturbation patterns not used in training and by comparing predicted absolute fluxes with independent total heat, current, mass-loss, or reaction-rate measurements. This closes the loop between local interfacial coefficients and macroscopic transfer rates, which is the key unresolved gap in triple-phase catalysis and evaporative or reactive interface studies [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4223928347)."
        }
